# [smoke]($heading.id('smoke'))

[`examples/smoke`](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/smoke): opens a window whose page calls into Zig to check every enabled module, then lists each check with what it verified. One run tells you Oriel works end to end on your machine, not just that it compiles.

- **Tray and menus**: dbusmenu icons, layout and actions.
- **Updater**: Ed25519-verified manifests; tampered copies rejected.
- **Data**: SQLite (with sqlite-vec), the JSON store, file watching.
- **Desktop**: dialogs, notifications, global shortcuts, clipboard, typed input into other apps.
- **Webview and IPC**: page fetch, `invoke` round trips, async commands from worker threads, and an iframe from another origin that must not reach IPC.

```sh
cd examples/smoke
zig build
./zig-out/bin/oriel-smoke              # the window
./zig-out/bin/oriel-smoke --auto-quit  # quits once the page reports; exit 1 on failure
./zig-out/bin/oriel-smoke --check      # headless: run the checks, print them, exit
```

CI runs it on every platform, so a red row names the module that broke.

[](<$image.siteAsset('screenshots/smoke.png').alt('The smoke test app: a table of green OK rows, one per module, each with what it verified')>)

# [breakout]($heading.id('breakout'))

[`examples/breakout`](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/breakout): Breakout for comparing the two renderers. The board is a `<canvas>` drawn at the display's pixel ratio; the score, overlays, settings and stats around it are HTML and CSS, so the native widgets get exercised too.

- **Two modes** on the native renderer (Settings → Renderer mode): **JS** steps `physics.js` and draws with `draw.js` each frame; **Zig** runs the same rules and drawing in `game.zig` through `oriel.canvas`, with no JavaScript per frame. The page keeps the HTML and the input.
- **Settings**: balls per launch (1 to 500), brick rows, particles, autoplay and a stats overlay with the frame rate, frame time and JavaScript time per frame.
- **Controls**: mouse or arrow keys on the desktop; drag on a phone.

With 500 balls on a 120 Hz phone: 120 fps from Zig (0.35 ms a frame), 63 fps from JavaScript, 120 in the WebView. On a 180 Hz desktop: 180 fps from Zig, 88 to 108 from JavaScript.

```sh
cd examples/breakout
zig build                                   # WebView
zig build -Dnative_ui -p zig-out-native     # native renderer
BREAKOUT_DEMO=500 BREAKOUT_MODE=zig ./zig-out-native/bin/oriel-breakout   # autoplay, logs fps
```

```=html
<figure class="smoke-shot breakout-shots doc-shot">
<img src="/Oriel/screenshots/breakout-desktop.png" alt="Breakout in the native renderer on Linux, Zig mode: bricks, balls and the stats overlay" loading="lazy">
<img class="phone" src="/Oriel/screenshots/breakout-phone.png" alt="Breakout in the native renderer on an Android phone, Zig mode, at 120 fps" loading="lazy">
<figcaption>The native renderer in Zig mode, on Linux and an Android phone</figcaption>
