```

# [system-monitor]($heading.id('system-monitor'))

[`examples/system-monitor`](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/system-monitor): a live CPU, memory and process monitor. The Zig side reads `/proc` directly, with no subprocess, and the page renders it in the WebView or the native renderer.

- **Stat tiles**: CPU, memory and process count with 60-sample sparklines, and uptime.
- **Toolbar**: pause and resume, filter by name or PID, sort by CPU, memory, PID or name.
- **Process table**: the top processes, with Terminate (a `SIGTERM` sent from Zig after a confirmation) and Copy (through the clipboard module).
- **Badges**: the renderer in use and how long each Zig sample took.

The page talks to Zig through four typed commands: `sample`, `terminate_process`, `copy_to_clipboard` and `get_meta`. Measured against the same app built with another native SDK, sampling is about three times faster and the native renderer build uses less memory and CPU; the numbers and method are on the [comparison page]($link.page('docs/comparison')).

```sh
cd examples/system-monitor
oriel run -Dnative_ui     # native renderer
oriel run                 # WebView
```

[](<$image.siteAsset('screenshots/system-monitor.png').alt('The system monitor on the native renderer: CPU, memory, processes and uptime tiles above a sortable process table')>)

*The native renderer on Linux, sampling every 2 s.*

# [render-bench]($heading.id('render-bench'))

[`examples/render-bench`](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/render-bench): one static page, timed in the WebView and in the native renderer. It builds and updates rows of styled text (each followed by a layout read), animates 200 boxes with `requestAnimationFrame`, runs a `<canvas>` game loop, and records memory. A **Measure power** button times idle, animation and canvas scenarios against the battery.

On a 180 Hz Linux desktop (median of 3 runs):

| | native | WebView |
|---|---|---|
| startup → first frame | 69 ms | 431 ms |
| build 1000 / 3000 rows | 6.4 / 18.6 ms | 20 / 69 ms |
| update 1000 / 3000 rows | 2.8 / 8.5 ms | 9 / 40 ms |
| animate 200 boxes | 165 fps | 62 fps |
| canvas 200 / 1000 balls | 180 / 91 fps | 62 / 62 fps |
| memory at start (PSS) | 110 MB | 491 MB |

```sh
cd examples/render-bench
zig build -Doptimize=ReleaseFast                                 # WebView
zig build -Dnative_ui -Doptimize=ReleaseFast -p zig-out-native   # native renderer
RENDER_BENCH=1 ./zig-out-native/bin/oriel-render-bench           # one JSON line, then exits
```

[](<$image.siteAsset('screenshots/render-bench.png').alt('Render bench in the WebView: row timing results and animated boxes')>)

Recordings of both renderers running it side by side are on the [native renderer page]($link.page('docs/native-renderer')).

# [canvas-demo]($heading.id('canvas-demo'))

[`examples/canvas-demo`](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/canvas-demo): a small page for the 2d canvas, with no commands on the Zig side. Three canvases:

- **Static**: filled and stroked shapes, a rotated rectangle, a curve and centered text.
- **Animated**: balls moving on a `requestAnimationFrame` loop, with the renderer and frame rate shown above.
- **Hostile**: calls a browser shrugs off (extra `restore()`, `scale(0)`, a coordinate that overflows f32, a `clearRect` over a red background). They must not break the window, and the text after it must still draw.

```sh
cd examples/canvas-demo
zig build && ./zig-out/bin/oriel-canvas-demo
zig build -Dnative_ui -p zig-out-native && ./zig-out-native/bin/oriel-canvas-demo
```

[](<$image.siteAsset('screenshots/canvas-demo.png').alt('The canvas demo in the WebView: shapes and text, moving balls, and the hostile canvas still drawing')>)
